2|0@nfl^1439857435#cu6i6pt:brobroma$:The majority is. The main core of the activists who actively protest the Redskins however (the #NotYourMascot crowd) want all Native imagery stripped out of sports mascots. It's just with the Redskins, you have the slur and fans often in headdresses or even redface so it gets more attention.
2|0@nfl^1439863173#cu6ldx4:superjuan$:&gt;I'm not aware of people being upset much about the Braves. If Washington changed the name and generally treated Native Americans with respect in their publicity I think it would be fine. [Yeah right....](http://i.imgur.com/eiEhPA9.gif) Sorry friend but [the problem is, and really has always been, more about American Indian imagery as a mascot in general](http://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/2014/05/04/redskins-mascot-nickname-indians-wahoo-chiefs-blackhawks-braves/8705159/). Sure the name "Redskins" is today's cause celebre, but it's naive to think it stops at just the name. Hell, I'm old enough to remember when the name Redskins was, comparatively, a non-issue. Even you mentioned how the issue used to be with caricatures like Chief Wahoo (which the Indians contemplated getting rid of, but didn't) and Chief Noc-A-Homa (which the braves got rid of in 1986). Back in the 80s and 90s I very distinctly remember thinking, "Well the Redskins don't have that problem because they have a dignified and realistic logo which was honorific in comparison." And they really didn't come after the Redskins, at least not for the logo. But now they're coming after the name and they've made it pretty clear that it's not just the name they have issues and teams like the [Chiefs](http://profootballtalk.nbcsports.com/2014/06/26/plaintiff-in-redskins-patent-case-urges-chiefs-to-change-their-name/) are next.
