1|0@nfl^1439907976#cu71t35:morecomplete$:Helicopter. Unlike animals or people such as Vikings or Irish, Native Americans are not ever to be used as mascots. Pay attention. 
1|0@nfl^1439894915#cu6wn2j:ctetc2007$:Which was named after Black Hawk of the Sauk nation. &gt;McLaughlin had been a commander with the 333rd Machine Gun Battalion of the 86th Infantry Division during World War I. This Division was nicknamed the "Blackhawk Division" after a Native American of the Sauk nation, Black Hawk, who was a prominent figure in the history of Illinois. McLaughlin named the new hockey team in honor of the military unit, making it one of many sports team names using Native Americans as icons. However, unlike the military division, the team's name was spelled in two words as the "Black Hawks" until 1986, when the club officially became the "Blackhawks," based on the spelling found in the original franchise documents. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago_Blackhawks#Founding In this case, it's Native American related, but I feel that the name is honoring the guy, not using his name as a pejorative. EDIT: On a similar note, how did the US military get away with naming all their helicopters something Native American related?
